  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1.Isolated showers continue along the coast this\nafternoon, with thunderstorms remaining well offshore. Winds\nslowly shifting to become easterly this afternoon, becoming\nvariable this evening then calm tonight. Tonight into tomorrow\nmorning, calm winds will bring a fog threat away from the coast,\nwhich could be locally dense just before/around sunrise. Next \nappreciable chance of rain arrives Mon and Tue as yet another \nfrontal boundary pushes southward. For Mon, the rain chances are\nconfined to mainly NW zones as the front has trended slower and\nlater in the day. Tue appears the best chance for widespread \nshowers and embedded thunderstorms as PW's surge to aoa 2\" and \nfrontal boundary is draped acrs ENC, and thus pops raised to \n60-70%.\n\nKey Message 2...By mid week, strong high pressure system \nbecomes anchored acrs New England and ern Canada, while at the \nsame time broad low pres develops off the Mid Atlantic coast, \ntightening the pressure gradient along the mid- atlantic and \nnortheast. This setup will usher in stronger NE winds into\ncoastal ENC. Exact timing, placement, and strength of the low\nwill dictate wind speeds and impacts to coastal ENC. Ensemble\nprobs still show potential for 20-30mph sustained winds, gusting\nto 40-50mph along OBX. This however is highly variable as it\ndepends on the low. What is certain is that a long fetch of \ntightened nerly gradient winds offshore will bring high wave \nenergy towards vulnerable portions of the Outer Banks. It is too\nsoon to determine specifics at this point and trends will have \nto be monitored in the coming days. There is high confidence in\nover 9 foot waves at over 9 second periods impacting the Outer\nBanks late this week.",
